## Proof

We evaluate

$$I = \int_0^{+\infty} \frac{\sin^4 x}{x^4}\,dx.$$

The proof proceeds by two integrations by parts, reducing $I$ to integrals of the form $\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^2 x}{x^2}\,dx$ and $\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^4 x}{x^2}\,dx$, which are then computed using the standard identity

$$\int_0^{+\infty} \frac{1-\cos ax}{x^2}\,dx = \frac{\pi a}{2}, \qquad a>0,$$

itself a direct consequence of the given $\int_0^{+\infty} \frac{\sin x}{x}\,dx = \frac{\pi}{2}$.

### Preliminary identity

For $a>0$, integrating $\int_0^\infty \frac{1-\cos ax}{x^2}\,dx$ by parts with $u=1-\cos ax$, $dv=x^{-2}dx$ (so $v=-1/x$, $du=a\sin ax\,dx$):

$$\int_0^\infty \frac{1-\cos ax}{x^2}\,dx = \underbrace{\left[-\frac{1-\cos ax}{x}\right]_0^\infty}_{=\,0} + a\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin ax}{x}\,dx = a\cdot\frac{\pi}{2} = \frac{\pi a}{2},$$

since the boundary term vanishes (at $\infty$ by boundedness of the numerator; at $0$ since $1-\cos ax \sim \tfrac{a^2x^2}{2}$) and $\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin ax}{x}\,dx = \int_0^\infty \frac{\sin u}{u}\,du = \frac{\pi}{2}$ via $u=ax$. $\quad(\star)$

### Step 1 — First integration by parts

Set $u=\sin^4 x$, $dv=x^{-4}dx$, so $v=-\frac{1}{3x^3}$ and $du=4\sin^3 x\cos x\,dx$:

$$I = \left[-\frac{\sin^4 x}{3x^3}\right]_0^\infty + \frac{4}{3}\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^3 x\cos x}{x^3}\,dx.$$

The boundary term vanishes: at $\infty$, $|\sin^4 x|/x^3\to 0$; at $0$, $\sin^4 x\sim x^4$ so $\sin^4 x/x^3\sim x\to 0$. Thus

$$I = \frac{4}{3}\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^3 x\cos x}{x^3}\,dx. \qquad(1)$$

### Step 2 — Second integration by parts

In (1), set $u=\sin^3 x\cos x$, $dv=x^{-3}dx$, so $v=-\frac{1}{2x^2}$ and

$$du = \bigl(3\sin^2 x\cos^2 x - \sin^4 x\bigr)\,dx = \bigl(3\sin^2 x - 4\sin^4 x\bigr)\,dx,$$

using $\cos^2 x = 1-\sin^2 x$. Hence

$$\int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^3 x\cos x}{x^3}\,dx = \underbrace{\left[-\frac{\sin^3 x\cos x}{2x^2}\right]_0^\infty}_{=\,0} + \frac{1}{2}\int_0^\infty \frac{3\sin^2 x - 4\sin^4 x}{x^2}\,dx,$$

where the boundary term again vanishes (at $0$, $\sin^3 x\cos x\sim x^3$). Substituting into (1):

$$I = \frac{4}{3}\cdot\frac{1}{2}\int_0^\infty \frac{3\sin^2 x - 4\sin^4 x}{x^2}\,dx = 2A - \frac{8}{3}B, \qquad(2)$$

where

$$A = \int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^2 x}{x^2}\,dx, \qquad B = \int_0^\infty \frac{\sin^4 x}{x^2}\,dx.$$

### Step 3 — Evaluate $A$

Integrate by parts with $u=\sin^2 x$, $dv=x^{-2}dx$ ($v=-1/x$, $du=2\sin x\cos x\,dx=\sin 2x\,dx$):

$$A = \underbrace{\left[-\frac{\sin^2 x}{x}\right]_0^\infty}_{=\,0} + \int_0^\infty \frac{\sin 2x}{x}\,dx = \int_0^\infty \frac{\sin u}{u}\,du = \frac{\pi}{2},$$

via $u=2x$. So $A = \dfrac{\pi}{2}$.

### Step 4 — Evaluate $B$

Using $\sin^4 x = \frac{3 - 4\cos 2x + \cos 4x}{8}$, and decomposing

$$3 - 4\cos 2x + \cos 4x = 4(1-\cos 2x) - (1-\cos 4x),$$

we get

$$B = \frac{1}{8}\left[4\int_0^\infty \frac{1-\cos 2x}{x^2}\,dx - \int_0^\infty \frac{1-\cos 4x}{x^2}\,dx\right].$$

Applying $(\star)$ with $a=2$ and $a=4$:

$$B = \frac{1}{8}\left[4\cdot\frac{\pi\cdot 2}{2} - \frac{\pi\cdot 4}{2}\right] = \frac{1}{8}\bigl[4\pi - 2\pi\bigr] = \frac{1}{8}\cdot 2\pi = \frac{\pi}{4}.$$

### Step 5 — Combine

Substituting $A=\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $B=\frac{\pi}{4}$ into (2):

$$I = 2\cdot\frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{8}{3}\cdot\frac{\pi}{4} = \pi - \frac{2\pi}{3} = \frac{\pi}{3}.$$

### Result

$$\boxed{\int_0^{+\infty} \frac{\sin^4 x}{x^4}\,dx = \frac{\pi}{3}}$$

